{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794666628,"nonce":"sCkgk6rSq63WXlTr1HHa94Isoy0=","message":"Design, on branch single-classes. The runtimes differ in two places only: the native calls with their conversions, and how an object owns its handle. So:\n\n- One native class a library, `Hxn\u003cLibrary\u003eNative`: hxn-gen writes a shell `@:build(hxn.Native.library(\"\u003clibrary\u003e\"))`, and the macro in the hxn haxelib fills it from the manifest for the runtime compiled. It presents one Haxe API on every runtime: `String`, `haxe.io.Bytes`, `haxe.Int64`, Haxe functions for lent callbacks, `hxn.AtomicInt`, and `hxn.Handle` for handles. On HashLink each function wraps an `@:hlNative` primitive declared with the interface's own abstract (`hl.Abstract\u003c\"xwindow_monitor\"\u003e`, which HashLink checks against the primitive's signature) and converts: text from UCS-2 bytes, bytes as pointer and length with the out length, handles cast to and from `hxn.Handle` (checked: HashLink accepts the cast between abstract types). On hxcpp each calls the C++ glue through an extern (`@:native(\"\u003clibrary\u003e_glue::...\")`, `@:include`, the `@:buildXml`), with bytes through `getData` and `ofData`.\n- `hxn.Handle` in the hxn haxelib: on HashLink an abstract, released by the VM as now; on hxcpp an object holding the raw pointer and the library's release function, with one finalizer that calls it. A native function that returns an owned handle wraps it there, after the call has returned. So no generated class has a finalizer or `hxnOwn`, and `hxnAdopt(raw)` is the same on both.\n- Then everything hxn-gen writes in Haxe is one set of ordinary classes with bodies: interface classes, records, enums, serializers, the future/stream/signal makers, the memory meter, the typed-exception reader, weak classes. Their only runtime-specific parts were the native calls and ownership, now behind the native class and `hxn.Handle`.\n- The Rust adapters, C header and C++ glue are unchanged.\n\nOrder: plain methods (scalars, text, bytes, data, handles, nullable handles, fallible) on HashLink and hxcpp against the counter fixture and the examples; then results over time, lent functions, cells, weak references and typed exceptions; then CPPIA (first trying a script against the host's class with -D dll_import), wasm, mobile; then package one class path and regenerate hxn-window.","files":null}]}